<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<Project Sdk="Dalamud.NET.Sdk/15.0.0">
  <PropertyGroup>
    <Version>0.4.0.0</Version>
    <Author>Deepseek</Author>
    <Name>Mountbook</Name>
    <Punchline>A collection book for every mount: how to obtain it, what it costs, what it sells for</Punchline>
    <Description>Every mount in the game in one window: search and filter (with operators like tradeable:y or patch:&gt;=6.1), order it, group it by expansion, patch or acquire method, and read it as a card grid or a dense sortable table.

The detail pane shows the game's artwork and flavour text, a "How to obtain" list where every row that names a duty, quest or achievement opens its page, a token price with your own progress ("12,000 Seafarer's Cowries - you have 3,400, 8,600 to go"), community rarity, and - for a tradeable mount, when you ask - the cheapest listing on your data center.

The plugin reads the Mount, MountTransient and Item sheets and your own unlocked-mount state, and it writes nothing to the game. The Ride button uses the same mount action you could press yourself; the whistle link is an ordinary chat item link. Its one network call is the market price you ask for.

Patch, obtain-source and community-rarity data is derived from FFXIV Collect (ffxivcollect.com), a volunteer community project, and credits it in the window and in this description; that data is used non-commercially, as its API terms require. Everything else - names, artwork, flavour text - is read from your own game's sheets.

The screenshots on this entry are drawn by the plugin's own preview scenes rather than captured from a session, so they describe the window itself and can be regenerated for any version: https://github.com/KazimeirzOdnowiciel/ffxiv-plugins/blob/main/docs/screenshots.md</Description>
    <RepoUrl>https://github.com/KazimeirzOdnowiciel/Deepseek-Kingdom</RepoUrl>
    <Tags>mounts;collection;mount guide;how to obtain;market board;prices;glasswork</Tags>

    <!-- Opts this project into warnings-as-errors, code-style enforcement and
         nullable from Directory.Build.props. Do not remove. -->
    <StrictConventions>true</StrictConventions>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!-- Lets the previews project draw the internal view classes against a golden image. -->
    <InternalsVisibleTo Include="Mountbook.Previews" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!--
      The obtain snapshot (patch, whistle item, source lines) generated at dev time by
      scripts/generate-mount-sources. The LogicalName is pinned here and mirrored by
      MountCatalog.ResourceName; letting MSBuild derive it would turn a folder rename into a
      runtime "missing resource" bug.
    -->
    <EmbeddedResource Include="Data/mount-sources.json"
                      LogicalName="Mountbook.Data.mount-sources.json" />

    <!--
      One file per interface language, all embedded under one pinned name family. Loc.Scan finds them by
      the ".Strings.<code>.json" suffix, so adding a language is adding a file here - no code change, which
      is the whole point of the exercise. The LogicalName family is pinned for the same reason
      mount-sources.json's is: a folder rename must not become a runtime "no strings" bug.
    -->
    <EmbeddedResource Include="Data/Strings/*.json"
                      LogicalName="Mountbook.Data.Strings.%(Filename)%(Extension)" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    Glasswork: real refracting glass behind the window and themed widgets in place of ImGui's.
    MIT, four prerelease packages, API 15. Ships ~900 KB of its own assemblies in the plugin zip.

    The TerraFX Reference is NOT optional and is NOT a package: the glass renderer compiles
    against the TerraFX.Interop.Windows that Dalamud itself ships. $(DalamudLibPath) is defined
    by Dalamud.NET.Sdk; Private=false keeps that 28 MB file out of the plugin.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="Glasswork" Version="0.1.0-beta.2" />
    <PackageReference Include="Glasswork.Glass" Version="0.1.0-beta.2" />
    <PackageReference Include="Glasswork.Backend" Version="0.1.0-beta.2" />
    <PackageReference Include="Glasswork.Dalamud" Version="0.1.0-beta.2" />
    <Reference Include="TerraFX.Interop.Windows">
      <HintPath>$(DalamudLibPath)TerraFX.Interop.Windows.dll</HintPath>
      <Private>false</Private>
    </Reference>
  </ItemGroup>
</Project>
